From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: A733 combo-PHY -- refuse a redundant DP alt-mode entry

[Recipe cut: the debug instrumentation and its state file are not carried; the
two hunks that reported the new fields there are gone.]

The Type-C mux callback ran the whole DP entry sequence every time it was
called with a DP pin assignment, including when the lanes were already
DisplayPort's and nothing had changed. It re-wrote the lane map and the four
lane inverts and then ran `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-dp-reset-sequence`'s reset staircase, which de-asserts
phy0_reset_n on a link that is up.

THAT THIS HAPPENS IS MEASURED, NOT SUSPECTED
--------------------------------------------

Mainline's DisplayPort alt-mode driver notifies unconditionally:
dp_altmode_attention() calls dp_altmode_notify(dp) with no mode-changed test
(drivers/usb/typec/altmodes/displayport.c), so typec_mux_set() is re-issued
with the same TYPEC_DP_STATE_C on every Attention the partner sends.

And the boot logs have carried the fingerprint all along without it
being read: every healthy boot shows exactly TWO handover lines about 1.8 s
apart -- 48.93 s / 50.74 s on 2026-08-18, 8.074 s / 9.886 s on 2026-08-20
(1.812 s). The first is the Enter Mode, the second is the Attention path
arriving here and re-running everything.

Harmless before training. Underneath a CR/EQ window it fails the training of
whoever is driving it -- and it fails the driver arm and the shell arm
together, which is exactly the signature of the 2026-08-20 failure that
this tree could not attribute (9 pass / 15 fail, both arms dead on one boot,
both alive after a power cycle, nothing physical touched).

WHAT THIS DOES
--------------

  - Remembers the mux state the entry sequence ran for (dp_entered_mode) and
    refuses a repeat of the same one while dp_active, counting the refusals
    and saying so in the log. A different pin assignment still enters; a
    return to USB/SAFE still clears dp_active and lets the next entry through.
  - HPD is deliberately NOT part of the test, because this callback does not
    consume it: HPD travels to the DP driver, which retrains through
    phy_configure(), and `phy-allwinner-a733-combophy-bracket-the-dp-rate-table` put the reset bracket inside phy_configure()
    itself. A re-plug therefore still gets its staircase from the path that
    owns it. That is the assumption this patch is most likely to be wrong
    about, and the falsifier below tests exactly it.
  - Records what a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq() answered (dp_handshake_ok) and
    reports it in the debug instrumentation's state file, next to dp_active with its text
    corrected. dp_active is left where it is and means what it meant -- "the
    lanes belong to DisplayPort" -- because that is what the phy_configure()
    guard and the teardown path need it to mean. What was wrong was not the
    flag but reading it as a handshake outcome; the outcome now exists
    separately, and it is the sequence's own return value, not an intention.
  - The orientation path (typec_switch_set) records the same outcome, so a
    staircase re-run there cannot leave a stale answer behind.

THE PROOF THIS OWES
-------------------

  1. The disturbance witness, which is already instrumented: on a healthy
     boot, handover lines counted before and after the training window. With this
     patch a boot that used to show two handover lines must show one plus at
     least one "requested again while it is already active" line, and the
     count across a training window must stay flat.
     Falsified by: two handover lines still appearing, i.e. the second call
     carrying a different mode than the first.
  2. A re-plug still brings the picture back. Unplug the DisplayPort cable at
     the sink, plug it in again, and the link retrains without a reboot.
     Falsified by: a black screen that a `echo 0 > link` recovers -- that
     would mean the mux staircase was load-bearing for hotplug and this guard
     took it away.
  3. The link still trains from cold exactly as before: 26 passes and 0 failures on the training test, the
     sink answering 77 77 01 01, a picture by eye.
     Falsified by: any regression against
     an earlier run on the same
     fixture.
  4. dp_handshake reads "completed" on a boot that trains, and "FAILED" on a
     boot where the staircase does not complete -- the second half is the one
     that makes it worth having, and it is only observable on a test kernel
     (the state file belongs to the DP debug-instrumentation patch, which is not carried here and defaults to n).

NOT PROVEN YET: that either defect caused the 2026-08-20 intermittency. Only
that the path exists, that it is taken on every healthy boot, and that it can
do what that failure looked like. Fixing it is worth doing on its own terms.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
--- a/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/allwinner/phy-sun60i-a733-combophy.c
@@ -236,6 +236,20 @@ struct a733_combophy {
 	 * a board that never enters DP alt mode never executes any of them.
 	 */
 	bool			dp_active;
+	/* The mux state the DP entry sequence last ran for, and how many
+	 * redundant repeats of it were refused. Mainline's DisplayPort
+	 * alt-mode driver notifies on every Attention with no mode-changed
+	 * test, so this callback is re-entered with the same pin assignment
+	 * while the link is up -- see a733_combophy_typec_mux_set().
+	 */
+	unsigned long		dp_entered_mode;
+	unsigned int		dp_reenter_refused;
+	/* What a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq() answered on that entry. dp_active
+	 * says the lanes belong to DisplayPort; this says the handshake behind
+	 * that claim completed. Two different questions, and a gate that reads
+	 * the first for the second reads an intention as an outcome.
+	 */
+	bool			dp_handshake_ok;
 	/* The DP lane map and lane inverts, and the USB values they displace.
 	 * Written when the mux hands the lanes to DisplayPort and put back when
 	 * it takes them away, so a port that never enters DP alt mode never
@@ -1714,7 +1728,7 @@ static int a733_combophy_typec_set(struct typec_switch_dev *sw,
 	 * staircase. Guarded by dp_active, so a USB-only port never gets here.
 	 */
 	if (cp->dp_active)
-		a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(cp);
+		cp->dp_handshake_ok = !a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(cp);
 	mutex_unlock(&cp->lock);
 
 	/* A non-zero return here aborts TCPM's attach, so failures are logged,
@@ -1845,6 +1859,7 @@ static int a733_combophy_typec_mux_set(struct typec_mux_dev *mux,
 			 */
 			a733_combophy_dp_lanes_leave(cp);
 			cp->dp_active = false;
+			cp->dp_handshake_ok = false;
 			dev_info(cp->dev,
 				 "DP alt mode left, COMB0 links back in reset; SuperSpeed does not return without a phy_exit()/phy_init() cycle\n");
 		}
@@ -1881,12 +1896,43 @@ static int a733_combophy_typec_mux_set(struct typec_mux_dev *mux,
 		goto out;
 	}
 
+	/* The lanes are already DisplayPort's and nothing about the request has
+	 * changed, so there is nothing to hand over. Re-running the entry from
+	 * here would take a working link down: dp_lanes_enter() rewrites the
+	 * lane map and inverts and dp_reset_seq() is the reset staircase,
+	 * which drops phy0_reset_n. If one of those arrives inside a CR/EQ
+	 * window it fails the training underneath whoever is driving it -- and
+	 * both arms at once, which is what a fixture defect looks like.
+	 *
+	 * That this arrives at all is not a hypothesis. dp_altmode_attention()
+	 * calls dp_altmode_notify() with no mode-changed test
+	 * (drivers/usb/typec/altmodes/displayport.c), so every Attention
+	 * re-issues typec_mux_set() with the same TYPEC_DP_STATE_C, and every
+	 * healthy boot logged carries exactly two handover lines about 1.8 s
+	 * apart. The second one is this path.
+	 *
+	 * A genuine change still gets through: a different pin assignment does
+	 * not match, and a return to USB/SAFE clears dp_active above. HPD is
+	 * not handled here at all -- it travels to the DP driver, which retrains
+	 * through phy_configure(), whose own reset bracket is where a re-plug
+	 * gets its staircase.
+	 */
+	if (cp->dp_active && mode == cp->dp_entered_mode) {
+		cp->dp_reenter_refused++;
+		dev_info(cp->dev,
+			 "DP alt mode pin assignment %c requested again while it is already active: link left alone (%u refused)\n",
+			 pin, cp->dp_reenter_refused);
+		goto out;
+	}
+
 	dev_info(cp->dev,
 		 "DP alt mode pin assignment %c: handing all four COMB0 lanes to DisplayPort\n",
 		 pin);
 	cp->dp_active = true;
+	cp->dp_entered_mode = mode;
 	a733_combophy_dp_lanes_enter(cp);
-	if (a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(cp))
+	cp->dp_handshake_ok = !a733_combophy_dp_reset_seq(cp);
+	if (!cp->dp_handshake_ok)
 		dev_err(cp->dev,
 			"DP link bring-up did not complete, see the handshake errors above\n");
 
